export default "<page path=\"index.html\">\nPortfolio\n\nanoushka\n</page>\n\n<page path=\"about.html\">\nAbout\n\n# Hi, I'm Anoushka\n\nI'm a product designer at Deploy. I build products from 0–1 and the systems that scale them . I care about accessibility, and I like untangling complex dashboards and data tools .\n\nProduct designer, San Francisco\n\n[image: Anoushka with her grey tabby cat Valentino]\n\nMe + Valentino That's me with the real Valentino\n\n## Experience\n\n- Now Deploy UX / UI Designer\n\n- 2025 Deploy (then Hive) UX / UI Design Intern\n\n- 2025 The Michigan Daily Product Designer\n\n- 2023 Pawsncare Freelance Graphic Designer\n\n- 2022 Entrupy Knowledge Ops / Authentication Specialist\n\n- 2021 inext Graphic Designer\n\n## Say hi\n\nOpen to new opportunities. Reach out if you wanna be friends or colleagues!\n\nBuilt with HTML, CSS and JavaScript, with help from Claude Code . Set in Libre Baskerville and Inter .\n</page>\n\n<page path=\"fun.html\">\nFun\n\n# Fun\n</page>\n\n<page path=\"projects/mensio.html\">\nMensio\n\n# Redesigning Mensio's Data Explorer for self-serve analysis\n\nRole\nOne of two product designers\nCo-owned every design decision\n\nCollaborators\nEnterprise Analytics team\nFrontend & backend engineers\n\nTimeline\nJune – September 2026\n\nScope\nUser Research\nProduct Design\nDesign QA, Acting PM\n\nTL;DR\nFull story\n\nOverview\n\n## Mensio is a powerful tool that tracks brand exposure across TV and social…\n\nBrands, teams and networks use it for sponsorship and ad intelligence. It watches every second of 185 TV networks and 2,000+ social handles, and catches 13,000+ brand logos at 99%+ accuracy.\n\nAdding metrics, before and after\n\n[image: Guided Setup Metrics step: TV Sponsorship [Visual] picked, with its usual metrics added as chips]\n[image: The old Add Metric dialog listing every metric in every group at once]\n\nBefore\nAfter\n\n## …but customers couldn't get the data out on their own.\n\nData Explorer showed every metric, grouping and filter at once. Customers didn't know which combination answered their question, so they leaned on Mensio's analysts to pull the data for them.\nWe rebuilt chart building into a new chart builder: six presets for recurring questions, and Guided Setup, a step-by-step wizard, for everything else. We shipped it in 14 weeks. The two of us pushed for guardrails on every filter, cut the entry points from three to two, and surfaced features nobody knew existed.\n\nWe rebuilt chart building around presets and Guided Setup, a step-by-step wizard, and shipped it in 14 weeks. The two of us pushed for guardrails, two entry points instead of three, and surfacing hidden features.\n\nStarting assumption\n\n## We assumed customers were our primary users. We were wrong.\n\nNew to Mensio, we planned to start with customer pain points. Our first stakeholder conversations changed that.\n\nWhat we assumed\nCustomers built their own charts, and their pain points would show us what to fix.\n\nWhat we found\nThere were no documented customer pain points. Analysts were doing the work, so their workflow was what we needed to study.\n\nSubject: RE: Retailer EMV by team\nTo: Mensio analytics team\nWe've cut the data down to only home games as we think this is the cleanest way to isolate / keep from double counting. Is this correct?\nIf the way we've cut the data for total EMV by team isn't the cleanest way to do it, could you please help us get there?\nConsultant, strategy firm\n\nI was playing around with the dashboard and used these filters to pull the data for each brand individually into Excel.\nEquivalent Media Value [Visual] 2025-08-01 – 2026-09-08 Teams (Bars) Leagues (Bar Segments) Brands › Brand A Networks (165) › A&E, ABC, ACC… Event Types › Home Games Asset Types (47) › Ball, Basketball… Program Sub-Genres (14) › NBA…\nI just wanted to double-check that I'm doing this correctly and that the numbers look right.\nBrand partner\nCustomer emails asking for one-off pulls\n\n[image: Every table requirement is met, but Fetch is disabled] [image: Hovering the info icon shows only: Invalid selection combinations] [image: Add Data Grouping with Network Owners checked] [image: Network Owners swapped for Brand Owners] [image: Fetch is enabled again] [image: The chart loads] [image: The table appears] [image: The chart is back to empty: nothing was saved]\n\nEven analysts got stuck: an analyst's session in the old explorer\n\nThe problem\n\n## Every custom chart went through an analyst.\n\nA one-line customer email became an eight-step process across two people and three tools.\n\nA custom request, before and after\n\nBefore: analyst-run\nAfter: customer-run\n\n8 steps · 2 people · 3 tools · ~1 work day\n\n6 steps · 1 person · 1 tool\n\nClient\nAnalyst\nManager\n1 in 3 come back\nNot involved\nNot involved\n\n1 Emails one line\n2 Follows up\n3 Call if too complex\n4 Builds the pull\n5 Reviews\n6 Exports, cleans in Excel\n7 Sends export\n8 Revises\n\n1 Picks a preset or Guided Setup\n2 Metrics\n3 Date range\n4 Data groupings\n5 Filters\n6 Review & Fetch\n\n[image: The old chart builder: an empty canvas with an Add button and a list of table requirements] 1 2 3\nThe old builder started from a blank canvas\n- One Add button for every metric, grouping, filter and date.\n- Nothing appears until the requirements are met.\n- The only guidance is a count: 1–10 metrics, 1–10 groupings, 1 date range.\n\n[image: The old chart builder with metrics, groupings and filters stacked as chips above the chart] 1 2 3\nFilters stacked up as chips, with no guidance on which go together\n- Every choice becomes a chip in one row, with nothing grouping related filters.\n- The requirements only count chips, so all three check off.\n- Fetch, top right, unlocks even if the filters don't belong together.\n\nImpact on analysts\n\n## Analysts spent most of their day fulfilling requests.\n\n~1 day\nend-to-end per request\n\n85%\nof an analyst's day: 50% clarifying and building requests, 35% exporting and cleaning (Analyst A)\n\n1 in 3\nrequests came back for rework\n\nImpact on the business\n\n## Customers couldn't see Mensio's value or potential unless they used it themselves.\n\nLow adoption Customers stopped using Mensio or relied on analysts.\n\nWeak renewals Buyers rarely used the product after the demo, so they couldn't speak to its value at renewal.\n\nCapped value Mensio's full value was only reachable through the analytics team.\n\nThe insight The process lived in analysts' heads, so the product should ask the questions an analyst would.\n\nThe insight\n\n## The process lived in analysts' heads.\n\nAccurate pulls depend on knowing which filters and exclusions go together. Adding teams, for example, requires home, neutral and non-game filters to avoid double-counting. None of it was documented.\n\nDesign goal Have the product ask the questions an analyst would.\n\nAnalyst interviews\n\n## We studied the analyst workflow to rebuild it for customers.\n\nSince analysts were doing the work, we studied their workflow to understand what customers actually received. We used Mensio daily and interviewed the media analysts who handled customer requests.\n\nWhat analysts told us\n\nRaw notes\nGrouped\n\nAnalyst A Analyst B\n\nFollow-up questions were consistent\nDate range, live vs. replay, competitors, asset breakdown. Customers rarely specified these upfront.\n\nAnalysts over-pulled data\nTo avoid missing something, they pulled extra and filtered it in Excel.\n\nErrors surfaced a month later\nFinding a missing filter took about an hour, plus explaining the change to the customer.\n\nCustomers didn&#x27;t understand the data model\nGroupings and filters, such as programs vs. episodes, caused the most confusion.\n\n“Customers would email us with one sentence that usually just included the metric and TV program or brand.”\n\n“We then have to follow up and ask for the date range, whether they want shoulder programming, whether they want live events…”\n\n“Sometimes it is too complex or there are too many questions, so we have to hop on a call to clarify.”\n\n“ 0–2 times , depending how specific they are in the original ask.”\n\n“Often we pull way more data than we eventually need because we are afraid to miss something.”\n\n“We export more data than we need and manually filter out what we don't need in Excel.”\n\n“ 35% of the day is spent exporting the data and manually cleaning up the data exports.”\n\n“Usually it helps to see 4/5 different versions of a pull to fully contextualize the results.”\n\n“The bulk of the time is when we have to go back and add a metric about a month later .”\n\n“Usually once in every three data requests.”\n\n“There is no checklist, so you are trying to remember off the top of your head…”\n\n“When you add teams, you want to add home, neutral site and non-game filters, but exclude away games to not double count.”\n\n“Understanding basic data groupings: Brands, Programs, Episodes, Asset Types, Logo Occurrences, Networks.”\n\n“Users often don't understand how to interpret the data when segmented in different ways.”\n\n“Clients only get the Excel usually because they don't know how to modify the chart.”\n\n“We don't want them to change anything by accident and ruin the chart.”\n\nThe analysts' follow-up questions became the structure of the wizard.\n\nWishlist to roadmap\n\n## We merged stakeholder requests and research findings into one roadmap.\n\nStakeholders brought a wishlist, led by a left-to-right flow. We validated each item against the interviews and prioritized what to build.\n\nThe stakeholders' left-to-right vision\n\nTheir reference\nTheir ask for Mensio\n\nStakeholders pointed to the Domino's Pizza Tracker: always show where you are.\n\nOne page. Options open up as you move left to right.\n\n[image: Domino's Tracker: Ordered, Prep, Bake, Quality Check and Ready, with Ordered lit up]\n\nDomino's Tracker. Image: Domino's media room\n\n- Data type\n\n- Metrics\n\n- Data groupings\n\n- Filters\n\n- Dates\n\nStakeholder wish Interview evidence What we built\n\nStreamlined left-to-right flow\nBuild a chart in sequence, not all at once\n\nSame questions, every request\nAnalysts asked the same follow-ups, in the same order\n\nGuided Setup\nFive sequential steps, from metrics to review\n\nShow only what's relevant\nGuide customers through filter choices\n\nHidden nuances in data\nFilters confused customers; 1 in 3 requests needed rework\n\nProgressive options\nEach step shows only what fits, with tooltips and guardrails\n\nOffer pre-set views\nAnswers to the questions customers ask most\n\nRepeated requests\nMost were a metric plus a brand or program\n\nSix presets\nBased on the most common use cases\n\nWe chose a wizard as the core pattern. It matched the stakeholders' left-to-right vision and mirrored how analysts already worked.\n\nCustomer groups\n\n## Customer groups shaped the wizard's two branches.\n\nStakeholder interviews with the analytics team sorted customers into four groups by how they use Mensio. From one entry point, people choose how to build a chart: a preset for recurring questions, or Guided Setup for everything else.\n\nFour customer groups, from most guidance to least\n\n-\n\nNew users & requesters\nSenior and junior users who email the team instead of learning the tool. Often the budget owners.\n\nBuilt for them\nGuided Setup\n\nGuidance needed\n~50% of users\n\n-\n\nHigh-involvement users\nUse Core Modules daily, and email the team for a custom pull every two weeks.\n\nBuilt for them\nPresets\n\nGuidance needed\n~15% of users\n\n-\n\nPower users\nEdit charts in their custom dashboards, but wouldn't build one from scratch.\n\nBuilt for them\nPresets Duplicate Chart Add to Dashboard\n\nGuidance needed\n~30% of users\n\n-\n\nSuper users\nRun about 100 queries a month in Data Explorer, but make mistakes they don't know about.\n\nBuilt for them\nGuardrails on every filter\n\nGuidance needed\n~5% of users\n\nNavigating the mess\n\n## Three flow variations led to our final wizard.\n\nFeedback from analysts, engineers and stakeholders shaped each version.\n\nThree paths → two\nFull page → modal\nSix steps → five\n\nStarted with\n[image: Discarded design: Guided Flow, Custom and Create Using Preset as three separate options]\n\nEnded with\n[image: Final design: choose Guided Setup or Preset Templates]\n\nPresets, Guided and Custom confused the analytics team. We folded Custom into Guided Setup as “Frequently Selected Options.”\n\nStarted with\n[image: Discarded design: the wizard as a full page, with six steps listed on the left]\n\nEnded with\n[image: Final design: the wizard in a modal, on the Metrics step]\n\nBackend constraints required a modal, so we redesigned the micro-interactions to avoid modal-on-modal.\n\nStarted with\n[image: Discarded design: step 5 of 6, Frequency and Schedule, inside the modal]\n\nEnded with\n[image: Final design: Schedule Report in the chart's nav bar]\n\nWe moved scheduling to the nav bar to keep the modal focused on building a chart.\n\nTesting\n\n## The analysts tested Guided Setup and the presets.\n\nThe analytics team tested Guided Setup and the six presets throughout the build. They had no major feedback: the workflow is streamlined, and the guardrails mean they no longer work from memory.\n\n~60%\nless analyst time per custom request\n\nThe solution\n\n## Six design decisions shaped the new chart builder.\n\n## Customers pick a preset, or follow Guided Setup's five steps.\n\nA custom chart now: 6 steps, 1 person, 1 tool\n\n- 1 Preset or Guided\n\n- 2 Metrics\n\n- 3 Date range\n\n- 4 Groupings\n\n- 5 Filters\n\n- 6 Review & Fetch\n\n01 One entry, two paths Six presets for common questions; Guided Setup for everything else.\n\n[image: Create New Chart, Chart Setup: Guided Setup or Preset Templates, then a chart style]\n\n02 A linear flow Metrics → Date range → Grouping → Filters → Review, showing only options that fit.\n\n[image: Guided Setup modal: the five steps down the left, with Data Groupings active]\n\n03 Smart defaults Picking a metric group pre-selects the usual metrics.\n\n[image: TV Sponsorship [Visual] selected, with its usual metrics added as pills at the top]\n\n04 Built-in guardrails Each filter explains its rules, like double-counting; invalid combinations are blocked.\n\n[image: Filters step: each filter explains its rule, such as Teams double-counting games between two selected teams]\n\n05 Hidden features, surfaced We found capabilities nobody knew existed, like multiple date ranges, and surfaced them with a Seasons date picker and scatterplot and heatmap presets.\n\n[image: Date Range step: compare multiple date ranges turned on, with two ranges added]\n\n06 Shortcuts for power users Duplicate Chart, Add to Dashboard and Schedule Report cut repeated work.\n\n[image: Chart page with Add to Dashboard, Schedule Report and Duplicate in the top bar]\n\nGuided Setup, step by step\n\nMetrics Date range Groupings Filters Review\n\n[image: Metrics step: In-Content and Paid metric categories, none picked yet]\n[image: Date Range step: a date range picked and added as a pill]\n[image: Data Groupings step: frequently selected options checked, other options collapsed below]\n[image: Filters step: frequently selected filters with values picked, each with a description of when to use it]\n[image: Review step: every selection summarised by section, each with an Edit button]\n[image: The fetched chart: a table of dummy rows for the chosen metrics, dates, groupings and filters]\n\nFetching data…\n\nTry it: pick a metric, click Next or a step, and scroll inside the modal.\n\nDesign x Engineering\n\n## We designed within the backend's rules.\n\nWe acted as our own PMs, worked within the rules for valid chart combinations, asked for a Seasons API, and triaged QA by priority across a 14-week build.\nIn parallel, we refreshed Mensio's dated UI: new colours, and components rebuilt on the latest shadcn components as we built the Data Explorer flow.\n\nDesign QA, triaged P0–P4: 34 issues\n\nImpact & reflection\n\n## Customers now build their own charts.\n\nThe new chart builder shipped in September 2026. Customers now pull their own data without going through an analyst. They compare date ranges in one chart instead of stitching charts together in Excel, and they know where the exclusion filters are and why they matter.\n\n1 day → minutes\nfrom question to chart\n\n2 → 1\npeople: the customer, with no analyst in the loop\n\n3 → 1\ntools: Mensio, instead of email, Data Explorer and Excel\n\nEarly signals\n\n−40%\ncustom data requests to analysts since launch\n\n3×\ncharts built by customers themselves\n\n~10 min\nfrom question to chart, down from about a day\n\nCustomer feedback “I pulled our season numbers myself in ten minutes. That used to take a day of back-and-forth with the analytics team.” Brand partner\n\nEncode expert knowledge into the product\nPresets, defaults and tooltips now ask what analysts used to ask over email.\n\nDesign with constraints, not against them\nWorking within the backend's rules shaped a simpler flow.\n\nWhat didn't work\nOur first versions: three entry paths, a full-page wizard and a scheduling step. Analysts caught each one early, and we iterated.\n\nWhat's next\nMeasure adoption across a full renewal cycle, and save charts as people build them so a reload never wipes their work.\n</page>\n\n<page path=\"projects/deploy-design-system.html\">\nDeploy Design System\n\n# Rebuilding Hive's design system for Deploy\n\nCollaborators\nDesign team\nFrontend engineers\n\nTimeline\nShipped October 2026\n\nScope\nDesign System\nProduct Design\nVisual Design\n\nOverview\n\n## Hive is now Deploy.\n\nIn October 2026, Hive rebranded as Deploy, an AI inference platform for developers and enterprises. The brand, platform and website changed at the same time.\nWe rebuilt the design system for the new brand. One system now serves both the Portal and the marketing site, and we worked with frontend engineers through handoff.\n\nthehive.ai → deploy.com\n\n[image: deploy.com homepage after the rebrand]\n[image: thehive.ai homepage before the rebrand]\n\nBefore\nAfter\n\nThe problem\n\n## Hive's system didn't fit Deploy\n\nIt was built for trust and safety dashboards. Deploy needed a system for a developer platform, a new website and a model catalog.\n\nAuditing Hive\n\n## What we started with\n\nHive had primitive and semantic tokens, but only for a light theme. There was no dark mode.\nThe type was Roboto, which made the products look dated. We took on the challenge of modernising them for Deploy.\n\nHive design system\n\nTypeface\n\nAa\n\nRoboto\nRegular, Medium, SemiBold\n\nHead 4 24 / 32\n\nHead 6 18 / 28\n\nBody 14 / 20\n\nCaption 12 / 16\n\nColour\n\nblue-500 #206CFD\nblue-400 #1853DF\ngreen-500 #3BC27C\nred-500 #D10A1E\npurple-500 #9699F5\nmagenta-700 #9923EC\ngrey-800 #333D47\ngrey-175 #EDEDEF\n\nButtons\n\nStyles\n\nFilled Outlined Ghost\n\nStates\n\nDefault Hover Disabled\n\nSizes\n\nXs Md Xl\n\nForms\n\nSample text\nEnter text here\nFocused field\nFilled field\nEnter text here\n\nError\n\nBadges\n\nPlayground available\nFeatured\nLLM\nMultimodal LLM\nEnterprise\n\nChips\n\nFlagged\nReview\nPattern\n\nExploration\n\n## Exploring color and type\n\nWe tested a palette and a type scale before settling on the final system.\n\nPalette\n\nExplored Final\n\nBackground #F5F5F5\nForeground #111E69\nPrimary #0047C2\nSecondary #00B877\nAccent #D3E151\n\nCreate project New\nMy Project\n\nPreview Deploy\n\nGoals\n\nDesign System\n\nMobile App\n\nMarketing Site\n\nContrast Ratio WCAG AA\n\nAccent on white 1.4:1 Fail\n\nSecondary on white 2.6:1 Fail\n\nWhite on primary 7.8:1 Pass\n\nBackground #FFFFFF\nForeground #0D111B\nPrimary #206CFD\nSecondary #F4F7FE\nAccent #E6EEFF\n\nCreate project New\nMy Project\n\nPreview Deploy\n\nGoals\n\nDesign System\n\nMobile App\n\nMarketing Site\n\nContrast Ratio WCAG AA\n\nForeground on secondary 17.6:1 Pass\n\nMuted text on white 4.8:1 Pass\n\nWhite on primary 4.5:1 Pass\n\nDropping the volt green\n\nThe volt green failed contrast on white and worked more as an accent than a secondary, so we kept blue as the primary.\n\nType scale\n\nProduct scale\n\nAa\ntext-lg Medium 18/24\nAa\ntext-sm Medium 14/20\nAa\ntext-xs Regular 12/16\nAa\ntext-xxs Regular 10/12\n\nBadge\n\nModel name\n\nShort model description.\n\nINPUT\n$0.00 / 1M\n\nOUTPUT\n$0.00 / 1M\n\nTry now ›\n\nMarketing scale\n\nAa\nH1 Medium 64/66, −4%\nAa\nH3 Medium 32/40, −4%\nAa\nH5 Semibold 18/24, −4%\nAa\nCaption Regular 12/16\n\nBadge\n\nModel name\n\nShort model description.\n\nInput Tokens\n$0.00 / 1M\n\nOutput Tokens\n$0.00 / 1M\n\nTag Try Now >\n\nKept from the first round\n\nBoth scales worked from the start, so we kept them. The Portal needs small, dense sizes that map to the Tailwind classes engineers use. The marketing site needs bigger headlines, so it uses its own H1–H6 scale.\n\nThe system\n\n## Foundations, components, themes\n\nPrimitive tokens hold the raw values. Semantic tokens point to them, so components can switch themes.\n\nFoundations\n\n## Primitive and semantic tokens\n\n-\nColor palette\nSix ramps, with blue as the primary.\n\nBlue (Primary)\n\n50 F4F7FE\n100 E6EEFF\n150 F8FAFD\n200 C7D9FF\n250 B4CCFF\n300 9FBCFF\n350 7DA6FF\n400 5C8FFF\n450 5169AC\n500 206CFD\n600 2457FF\n700 174BCC\n800 123AA3\n900 0D2A78\n950 071D58\n975 121C3A\n\nGrey\n\n100 F1F5F9\n150 EDEDEF\n200 E2E8F0\n300 CBD5E1\n400 94A3B8\n500 64748B\n600 475569\n650 2B3644\n700 1F2937\n750 1E293B\n800 121723\n900 0D111B\n950 050811\n\nGreen\n\n50 F0FDF4\n100 DBF3E6\n200 BBF7D0\n300 86EFAC\n400 4ADE80\n500 22C55E\n600 0BA74F\n700 15803D\n800 166534\n900 14532D\n950 062E16\n\nOrange\n\n50 FFFBEB\n100 FEF3C7\n200 FDE68A\n300 FCD34D\n400 FBBF24\n500 F59E0B\n600 D97706\n700 B45309\n800 78350F\n900 7C2D12\n950 501602\n\nRed\n\n50 FEF2F2\n100 FEE2E2\n200 FECACA\n300 FCA5A5\n400 F87171\n500 EF4444\n600 D92323\n700 B91C1C\n800 991B1B\n900 7F1D1D\n950 490606\n\nPurple\n\n100 F0DEFC\n700 9923EC\n\n-\nSemantic colors\nOne set of names with a light and a dark value.\n\nSame token names, two values\n\nTitle\n\nDescription in muted text.\nPlaceholder\n\nSuccess Warning Error\n\nLink Secondary Primary\n\n- Backgrounds / Primary #FFFFFF #050811\n- Backgrounds / Secondary #FAFCFF #151B2D\n- Borders / Default #CBD5E1 #475569\n- Text / Primary #0F172A #FAFCFF\n- Text / Muted #64748B #94A3B8\n- Text / Brand (Link) #1D4ED8 #7DA6FF\n- Actions / Primary #206CFD #206CFD\n- Status / Success text #15803D #86EFAC\n\n-\nTypography\nInter, from text-xxs to text-9xl.\n\nInter\nClasses · size / line height · weights\n\ntext-9xl The quick brown fox 64/66 · Medium\ntext-8xl The quick brown fox 60/60 · Medium\ntext-7xl The quick brown fox 52/60 · Medium\ntext-6xl The quick brown fox 48/54 · Regular\ntext-5xl The quick brown fox 36/40 · Medium\ntext-4xl The quick brown fox 32/40 · Medium\ntext-3xl The quick brown fox 30/36 · Medium\ntext-2xl The quick brown fox 24/28 · Medium\ntext-xl The quick brown fox 20/28 · Medium\ntext-lg The quick brown fox 18/24 · Semibold, Medium, Regular\ntext-base The quick brown fox 16/26 · Semibold, Medium, Regular\ntext-sm The quick brown fox 14/20 · Semibold to Light\ntext-xs The quick brown fox 12/16 · Semibold to Light\ntext-xxs The quick brown fox 10/12 · Medium to Light\n\n-\nSpacing, radius and scale\nA 4px spacing base and an 8px radius base.\n\nSpacing\nBase 4px\n\nspacing-0 0\nspacing-0.5 2\nspacing-1 4\nspacing-1.5 6\nspacing-2 8\nspacing-2.5 10\nspacing-3 12\nspacing-4 16\nspacing-5 20\nspacing-6 24\nspacing-8 32\nspacing-10 40\nspacing-12 48\nspacing-16 64\nspacing-20 80\nspacing-24 96\n\nRadius\nBase 8px\n\n4px\n6px\n8px\n10px\n14px\n16px\n20px\n24px\nFull\n\nScale\nShared sizes, px\n\n#0 0\n#25 1\n#50 2\n#100 4\n#150 8\n#200 10\n#250 12\n#300 16\n#350 20\n#400 24\n#450 28\n#500 32\n#550 36\n#600 40\n#650 44\n#700 48\n#750 56\n#800 64\n\n-\nEffects\nElevation, focus rings, inner shadows and a glow.\n\nElevation\n\nfocus-ring\nfocus-ring-error\nshadow-grey-xs\nshadow-grey-sm\nshadow-grey-md\nshadow-grey-lg\nshadow-grey-xl\nshadow-grey-2xl\nshadow-glow\n\nInner shadows\n\ninner-shadow-subtle-light\ninner-shadow-pressed-light\ninner-shadow-strong-light\ninner-shadow-subtle-dark\ninner-shadow-pressed-dark\ninner-shadow-strong-dark\n\nComponents\n\n## 33 components, 977 variants\n\nBuilt on shadcn/ui and restyled with Deploy's tokens. Every component works in light and dark mode.\n\nThe basics\n\nTry it: hover, click or tab through the components.\n\nButton\n\nType Primary Outline Secondary Ghost Destructive Link\n\nSize Extra small Small Default Large\n\nInput\n\nSelect\n\nSelect\n\n- My Account\n\n- Profile\n\n- Billing\n\n- Settings\n\n-\n\n- Github\n\n- Support\n\nBadge\n\nDefault\nSecondary\nOutline\nSecondary dark\nDestructive\nSuccess\nWarning\n\nTabs\n\nOverview Settings Usage\n\nComplex components\nSelection, overlays, feedback and navigation.\n\nSelection\n\nTry it: pick one or more frameworks, or pick a date range.\n\nCombobox\n\nBasic\n\nSelect framework...\n\n- Next.js\n\n- React\n\n- Vue.js\n\n- Angular\n\n- Svelte\n\nMultiple\n\nSelect framework...\n\n- Next.js\n\n- React\n\n- Vue.js\n\n- Angular\n\n- Svelte\n\nDate Range Picker\n\nDate Range Picker\n\nOverlays\n\nTry it: open the dialog, drawer or popover.\n\nDialog, Drawer and Popover\n\nOpen Dialog\nOpen Drawer\n\nOpen Popover\n\nDimensions\nSet the dimensions for the layer.\n\nWidth\nMax. width\nHeight\nMax. height\n\nEdit profile\nMake changes to your profile here. Click save when you're done.\n\nName\nUsername\n\nCancel Save Changes\n\nEdit Profile\nMake changes to your profile here. Click save when you're done.\n\nName\nUsername\n\nSave Changes Cancel\n\nFeedback\n\nTry it: hover a type to show its toast.\n\nToast\n\nSuccess Info Warning Error\n\nAlert\n\nPayment successful\nA receipt was sent to your email.\n\nNew feature available\nDark mode is now in Settings.\n\nPayment failed\nCheck your payment method.\n\nSubscription expiring\nRenew within 3 days to keep access.\n\nNavigation\n\nTry it: open a menu, or click a page in the breadcrumb.\n\nNavigation Menu\n\nBreadcrumb\n\n- Home\n- Components\n- Breadcrumb\n\n- Home\n-\n\n- Docs\n- Guides\n- Components\n- Breadcrumb\n\nButton\n216 variants across type, size and state, including icon buttons.\n\nButton, every variant\n\nTry it: pick a component and size.\n\nButton Icon Button\n\nXS SM MD LG\n\nButton\n\nWith Icon\nNew Branch\n\nSpinner\nGenerating Downloading\n\nDesign x Engineering\n\n## Handoff to engineering\n\nType styles are named after the Tailwind classes engineers use, so specs map straight to code.\n\nToken naming and handoff\n\nCollections\n\nGroups\n\nImpact & reflection\n\n## Shipped with the rebrand\n\n- Deploy launched in October 2026 on the new system.\n\n- Light and dark themes from one set of tokens.\n\n- Separate type scales for product and marketing.\n\nCheck contrast early\nThe volt green failed before it reached a single screen.\n\nName tokens the way engineers do\nMatching Tailwind names made handoff simpler.\n</page>\n\n<page path=\"projects/denials.html\">\nClaim Denials\n\n# Redesigning a denials platform so revenue cycle teams go from alert to fix faster\n\nRole\nProduct designer on a student team\nShared the design work\n\nTeam\nAnna, Anoushka, Ashita, Deeksha\n\nClient\nA healthcare revenue cycle company\nUniversity of Michigan capstone\n\nTimeline\nSeptember – December 2025\n\nScope\nUser Research\nProduct Design\nPrototyping\n\nTL;DR\nFull story\n\nOverview\n\n## Hospitals lose money every time an insurer denies a claim…\n\nOur client's platform helps hospital revenue cycle teams track denied claims, find the root cause, and stop the same denials from coming back. It groups denials into cases. Teams investigate each case, then open an intervention to fix the cause.\nThe client's own users called the interface clunky, and they were planning a new version. For our University of Michigan mastery course, our team spent a semester redesigning it with them.\n\nThe case page, before and after\n\n[image: Redesigned case page: five score cards, a bar chart with the June spike linked to the comment that explains it, and the root cause beside it]\n[image: Wireframe of the old case page: a header of same-size numbers, eight tabs, and a line chart with nothing explaining its spike]\n\nBefore\nAfter\n\n## …but finding out why took too many clicks, filters typed by hand, and a trip to Excel.\n\nWe rebuilt the four screens on the path from an alert to a fix: the dashboard, the cases list, the case page and starting an intervention. The thread through all of them was the same. Show the dollar impact first, keep the next step on the screen you're on, and let people filter like they do in a spreadsheet.\n\nWe rebuilt the dashboard, cases list, case page and intervention flow around one idea: show the dollar impact first, and keep the next step on the screen you're on.\n\nWho uses it\n\n## People whose job is to get denied claims paid, and to stop them happening again.\n\nDenial specialists\nWork the cases: investigate a spike, find the root cause, open an intervention.\n\nA/R follow-up and billing teams\nPick up interventions for their region and billing type, and close them out.\n\nRevenue cycle leaders\nNeed to see trends, team progress and the dollars at stake, without digging.\n\nThe client's success team\nWalk customers through the platform and pass on what they hear.\n\nResearch\n\n## We asked how people handle denials today, before looking at the screens.\n\nOur professor pushed us to step back from the demo environment. A usability test shows where people get stuck, but not what they're trying to find out, so we needed users to tell us the story of their last denial.\nWe ran five sessions with the client's stakeholders and users, two of them usability tests of the current product. I scheduled the client sessions. We then mapped every interaction, ran a heuristic evaluation, sorted the findings in an affinity map, and did a cognitive walkthrough with the client's product owner, from an alert to a resolved denial.\n\n5\nsessions with stakeholders and users\n\n2\nusability tests of the current product\n\n1\ncognitive walkthrough, alert to resolution\n\nWhat the tests looked at\n\nSpotting trends\nHow people find and investigate a denial spike in the charts.\n\nFiltering cases\nHow they filter and organize cases in the tables.\n\nKey workflows\nHow easily they finish the core denial-management tasks.\n\nGrouping\nHow they group and review related cases.\n\nThe problem\n\n## The data was there. Getting to it was the work.\n\n“It's a lot of clicking.”\nClient user\n\n“Typing filters manually is tedious.”\nClient user\n\n“I'd need Excel to really understand this.”\nClient user\n\n“It almost feels like I'm documenting things twice.”\nClient user\n\nOur heuristic evaluation put the same issues on the screens. These wireframes show the old layouts, redrawn without the client's interface.\n\n[image: Wireframe of the old cases list: a typed filter field, twelve columns and a CSV button] 1 2 3\nCases list\n- Filters were typed by hand, as text.\n- Twelve columns, with the dollar change far to the right.\n- To rank by dollar impact, people exported to CSV and sorted in Excel.\n\n[image: Wireframe of the old case page: a header of numbers, eight tabs and a line chart] 1 2 3\nCase page\n- Every number at the same size. The dollar impact didn't stand out.\n- Eight tabs. Comments and notes sat on different tabs from the chart.\n- A spike in the chart, with nothing next to it explaining why.\n\nThe insight People weren't missing data. They were missing a short path from “what spiked?” to “who's fixing it?”\n\nPrinciples\n\n## Four rules for every screen.\n\nDollars first\nLead with the dollar impact, so the costliest denials get worked first.\n\nFilter like a spreadsheet\nFilter and sort from the column, instead of typing filters.\n\nNext step on the same screen\nFewer clicks between spotting a problem and acting on it.\n\nSame cues everywhere\nOne layout and one set of controls across cases, interventions and reports.\n\nThe redesign\n\n## Every complaint got an answer on the screen.\n\nPick a quote to see the part of the redesign that answers it. All screens are recreations of our designs with made-up data.\n\n“It's a lot of clicking.”\n“Typing filters manually is tedious.”\n“I'd need Excel to really understand this.”\n“It almost feels like I'm documenting things twice.”\n\nDashboard\n\n## Goal: fewer steps, clearer insights, faster decisions.\n\nThe old home stacked numbers, lists and charts in one long column. Hover the markers to see what each part of the new one does.\n\n[image: Redesigned dashboard: a band of case, intervention and task cards, then a weekly cases chart and a status breakdown]\nJul 2\nPriority 30 · Regular 26 1 2 3 4\nThe new dashboard\n- Totals and the high-priority list share a card, so the most urgent case is one click from home.\n- My tasks keeps each person's follow-ups on the home screen.\n- Hover a bar for its priority and regular counts, instead of reading them off the axis.\n- Status reads as one stacked bar with counts, so the backlog shows at a glance.\n\nCases\n\n## Goal: find and filter key cases quickly.\n\nFilter by any column, sort from any header, and choose which columns to show. The clip below plays on its own; click into it to try it.\n\nClick in to try it\n\nCase page\n\n## Goal: see the spike and the reason together.\n\nThe header went from a row of equal numbers to five score cards, with the dollar change outlined. Comments moved out of their tab and next to the chart: each one links to the month it explains, and the root cause sits right under them. Drag the slider at the top of the page to compare.\n\nBefore → after 11 numbers → 5 score cards. 8 tabs → 1 page.\n\n[image: Old case page, outlined: eleven same-size numbers in the header, and Team comments as one of eight tabs]\nBefore. 11 same-size numbers, and comments on their own tab.\n\n[image: New case page, outlined: five score cards with the dollar change highlighted, and comments beside the chart]\nAfter. 5 score cards, and comments beside the chart.\n\nInterventions\n\n## Goal: reduce repetition and guide people through it.\n\n“Documenting things twice” came from the hand-off between a case and its intervention. Starting one is now four short steps with a progress bar, each saying what it's for. Carrying the case's notes forward is our top recommendation for the next version.\n\n[image: Step 1 of 4: name the intervention]\n1. Name it. The progress bar shows where you are.\n\n[image: Step 2 of 4: set the region]\n2. Set the region. Cases are chosen after region and billing type.\n\n[image: Step 3 of 4: set the billing type]\n3. Set the billing type. So the right team picks it up.\n\n[image: Step 4 of 4: confirm the root cause]\n4. Confirm the root cause. Required once a case moves to intervention.\n\n[image: Intervention created, with its name, region, billing type and root cause]\nDone. One summary line instead of a second write-up.\n\n← →\n\nTry it yourself\n\n## From alert to fix, in four screens.\n\n1. Spot\n2. Find\n3. Understand\n4. Fix\n\nSpot it: hover the bars to see priority and regular cases for each day.\n\nImpact & reflection\n\n## A shorter path from “what spiked?” to “who's fixing it?”\n\nWe handed the client a clickable prototype, marked-up designs with the reasons behind each change, and a final report. For users, the redesign means fewer clicks and a clear next step. For the client and its customers, it means more accurate prioritization and faster work on high-dollar denials.\n\n4\nWorkflows redesigned: dashboard, cases, case page and intervention\n\n4\nUsability tests: two on the old product, two on our first designs\n\n18 mo\nImplementation plan handed to the client\n\nStart with the story, not the screen\nAsking about the last denial someone worked told us more than watching them click.\n\nWhat's next\nMeasure time to find a high-priority case, time to filter and start an intervention, and how often analysts still export to Excel.\n</page>\n\n<page path=\"projects/ctrlz.html\">\nctrl z\n\n# ctrl z: plan a day you can't predict\n\nAn anti-algorithm app. Real people, not a feed, plan your day, and you see it one surprise card at a time.\n\nReplay\n\nPause\n0:00 Sound on\n\nEvent Converge '25, University of Michigan\n\nTime 2½ hours\n\nResult 1st place 🏆\n\nTeam Spaghetti Amulya, Anoushka, Ayana, Deeksha, Dhanush\n\nMy part\n\n- The swipe. I designed the swipe feature: right on what you're up for, left on what you're not.\n\n- How it works. I owned the app's logic, from preferences to the day cards and what happens when you say nah.\n</page>\n\n<page path=\"projects/bumpy.html\">\nBumpy AI\n\n# Bumpy AI: a pregnancy helper you can just ask\n\nAn AI tab for a pregnancy app. Ask a question and the answer turns into a plan: stretches, appointments, food checks, a daily list.\n\nReplay\n\nPause\n0:00 Sound on\n\nEvent Business+Tech Innovation Jam, Michigan Ross\n\nTime 6 weeks, 2025\n\nTeam Aishwarya, Anoushka, Isaac, Net\n\nMy part\n\n- The only designer. The other three came from business school; everything you see, I designed.\n\n- The flows. How a question becomes a plan, from the chat to the Plan tab.\n\n- Brand, screens and prototype. The footprint logo, colour and type, every screen, and the clickable prototype for the pitch.\n</page>\n\n<page path=\"projects/somnia.html\">\nSomnia\n\n# Somnia: wind down and keep a steady bedtime\n\nA sleep app for college students. It nudges you toward the same bedtime every night, walks you through winding down, and plants a seed in your garden for every calming activity.\n\nReplay\n\nPause\n0:00 Sound on\n\nCourse Consumer Health Technologies, UMSI\n\nTime Fall 2025\n\nTeam Anoushka, Ciani, Feyi, Jillian, Tolani\n\nMy part\n\n- Brainstorming. I ran the team's brainstorming and came up with the ideas we built on.\n\n- The de-stressing activities. I designed the Playground: Capture your moment, Collect your calm and Recharge ritual.\n\n- Their interactions. The breathing circle, and the timed stretch with its progress bar and cheers.\n\n- The garden. Our professor asked us to keep the routine from feeling mundane, so I designed a reward: every activity plants a seed, seven seeds grow a flower, and staying consistent unlocks new gardens.\n</page>\n\n<page path=\"projects/coverflow.html\">\nCoverflow\n\n# Coverflow: browse Apple Music on Vision Pro one album at a time\n\nA concept that brings Coverflow back to Apple Music on visionOS. Your albums stand in a row in your room, you flip through them, and playing a song can turn the room into its world.\n\nClick the Coverflow button, flip through the songs and click one to play its preview. Formula 1 Theme takes you into the race; steer with your pointer or the arrow keys. Song previews come from Apple Music. Open it full screen\n\nCourse Intro to Product Management, UMSI\n\nTime 2025\n\nTeam Solo, built on a visionOS kit\n\nMy part\n\n- The idea. This class was the first time I tried the Vision Pro. I wanted to bring back Coverflow, the old way of flipping through album covers, in a space where covers can really stand in front of you.\n\n- Coverflow in 3D. I designed the row of floating covers and how the library window clears to make room for them.\n\n- The Coverflow icon. An inactive and an active state for switching views.\n\n- Immersive mode. When the Formula 1 theme plays, the room turns into the track.\n\n- The prototype. Every screen and transition in the flow, built on a visionOS UI kit.\n</page>\n\n<scripted_answers note=\"Anoushka's own answers, written for Valentino\">\n- On deploy chat, deploychat, chatbot, chat product: With the rebrand, Deploy pivoted to being an inference company that hosts open-source LLMs. Deploy Chat is the obvious next big step, and she's designing it right now. It isn't public yet, so that's all I can say.\n- On hardest, difficult, challeng, toughest: The Mensio wizard flow. Even the team didn't know how to make the tool work efficiently, so she used every tool and method she could to understand the product and what it could do. Then she streamlined it so customers could pull their own data without reaching out to the analyst team. [Read the case study](projects/mensio.html).\n- On proud, favorite project, favourite project, best project: Mensio. Customers relied on analysts to pull their data, and even the team wasn't sure how the tool should work. She turned it into a guided flow customers can use on their own, and it shipped in 2026. [Read the case study](projects/mensio.html).\n- On mensio, data explorer, analytics, dashboard: Mensio is sponsorship and ad intelligence for brands, teams and networks. Customers didn't trust themselves to pull data, so they emailed analysts instead. She redesigned the Data Explorer so they can build it themselves. [Read the case study](projects/mensio.html).\n- On design system, hive, rebrand, landing: When Hive rebranded as Deploy in October 2026, Anoushka and the design team rebuilt the design system for the new brand. One system serves both the Portal and the marketing site: the color tokens are shared, and product and marketing each get their own type scale. She worked with frontend engineers through handoff. [Read the case study](projects/deploy-design-system.html).\n- On denial, claim, capstone, revenue cycle: For her University of Michigan capstone, Anoushka and three teammates redesigned a claim denials platform for a healthcare revenue cycle company, so teams get from an alert to a fix faster. They interviewed users, ran usability tests, redesigned four workflows and wrote an 18-month implementation plan. [Read the case study](projects/denials.html).\n- On ctrl z, ctrlz, ctrl-z, converge: ctrl z is an anti-algorithm app: real people, not a feed, plan your day, and you see it one surprise card at a time. Her team of five designed it in 2½ hours at Converge '25 and won 1st place. She designed the swipe and owned how the app works. [See ctrl z](projects/ctrlz.html).\n- On bumpy, pregnan, ross, tech jam: Bumpy AI is an AI tab for a pregnancy app: ask a question and the answer turns into a plan. It's from the Michigan Ross Business+Tech Innovation Jam. She was the only designer on a team of four, so she did the flows, brand, screens and prototype. [See Bumpy AI](projects/bumpy.html).\n- On somnia, sleep, bedtime, garden: Somnia is a sleep app that helps college students keep a steady bedtime. It's from a Consumer Health Technologies class at UMSI. She led the brainstorming and designed the de-stressing activities, their interactions and the garden that rewards them. [See Somnia](projects/somnia.html).\n- On coverflow, cover flow, vision pro, visionos: Coverflow brings Coverflow back to Apple Music on Vision Pro: your albums stand in a row in your room, and playing the Formula 1 theme turns the room into the track. She made it for Intro to Product Management at UMSI, the first time she tried a Vision Pro. [See Coverflow](projects/coverflow.html).\n- On fun project, side project, hackathon, jam: Four, from design jams and class: [ctrl z](projects/ctrlz.html) won 1st place at Converge '25, [Bumpy AI](projects/bumpy.html) is an AI helper for a pregnancy app, [Somnia](projects/somnia.html) is a sleep app that grows a garden, and [Coverflow](projects/coverflow.html) brings Coverflow to Apple Music on Vision Pro. [See the Fun page](fun.html).\n- On project, case stud, see first, start: Start with [Mensio](projects/mensio.html). It shows her research, product design and acting as PM on one project. Then see the [Deploy Design System](projects/deploy-design-system.html) for systems and visual design, and [Claim Denials](projects/denials.html) for research-led healthcare work. Her side projects are on the [Fun page](fun.html).\n- On freelance, contract, part-time, part time: She's only looking for full-time roles right now, so no freelance or contract work. [Book a chat](https://calendly.com/noushka-umich/30min) if you have a full-time role in mind.\n- On open to, hire, hiring, job: Yes, she's open to full-time roles. She'd love a design engineer kind of role, where she can use AI to design faster and more accurately. [Book a chat](https://calendly.com/noushka-umich/30min) or reach out to her on [LinkedIn](https://www.linkedin.com/in/anoushkaghosh/).\n- On contact, reach, email, mail: [Book a 30-minute chat](https://calendly.com/noushka-umich/30min), [email her](mailto:noushka@umich.edu) or message her on [LinkedIn](https://www.linkedin.com/in/anoushkaghosh/). Her code is on [GitHub](https://github.com/ghoshanoushka) and older visual work is on [Behance](https://www.behance.net/Anoushka-Ghosh).\n- On use ai, ai in, ai for, with ai: She uses AI across her whole workflow. She builds prototypes, and this site, with Claude Code. She has Claude work in Figma with her own components, like rebuilding charts or making dark-mode versions, and run design QA passes over her frames. She also uses it to turn research notes into themes. AI takes the repetitive parts, so she spends her time on the design decisions.\n- On tool, figma, figjam, miro: Figma, mostly, for design and prototyping. She uses FigJam and Miro for mapping and workshops, Adobe apps for visual and brand work, and other prototyping tools when a flow needs more than Figma. For building, she codes with Claude Code, which is how this site was made. Her code is on [GitHub](https://github.com/ghoshanoushka).\n- On engineer, pm, product manager, collaborat: She brings PMs and engineers in early: rough flows get walked through before they're polished, and every state is written down before handoff. On Mensio she also stepped in as PM, and she does design QA with engineers before anything ships.\n- On process, approach, how does she design, how she design: Understand first, then design. She talks to users and the people who know the product best, maps how it really works, then designs in small steps and tests until the flow feels obvious.\n- On for fun, hobb, free time, spare time: Lately she's been building a lot with Claude (token maxing, she calls it). Otherwise she's watching TV, cooking, or taking me and my brother Ollie out on walks.\n- On food, eat, cook, spice: Anything Indian, or anything that can match its level of spice and flavour. She cooks a lot too.\n- On from, kolkata, calcutta, india: She's from Kolkata, the City of Joy, in India. She moved to the US in 2024 for her master's and now lives in San Francisco.\n- On study, studied, degree, school: She studied fashion technology for her undergrad, then did her MS in HCI at the University of Michigan in Ann Arbor. Go Blue!\n- On into design, why design, why does she design, become a designer: She's always been passionate about designing for a cause. She wants to give something back to the world, creatively.\n- On experience, worked, work before, where: Anoushka is a UX/UI designer at Deploy, after interning there in 2025, when it was still Hive. Before that, she was a product designer at The Michigan Daily, an authentication specialist at Entrupy, a graphic designer at inext and designed social media for Pawsncare. [See her resume](https://drive.google.com/file/d/1x3pUb8lTMzYpkzIJ6nRIZ6F23U4mlWyp/view).\n- On skill, good at, strength, speciali: Anoushka covers user research, product design, design systems and visual design, plus design QA with engineers. She cares most about accessibility and inclusive design, and likes untangling complex dashboards and workflows.\n- On ollie, brother, sibling, other cat: Ollie is my brother. He's from India and flew all the way here! We're bonded, but between us, I don't mind when he's not around. He doesn't respect my space, and when he grooms me he chews my whiskers off.\n- On are you real, you real, real cat, real valentino: Yes, I'm real. I'm Anoushka's grey tabby. There's a photo of us on her [About page](about.html).\n- On your name, named, called, how old: I'm Valentino, Anoushka's grey tabby, and I turned 2 this year. She adopted me from a rescue in Ann Arbor. I came with the name, and since she picked me up on Valentine's Day, she kept it.\n- On you like, what are you like, personality, lazy: I'm the goodest boy. More dog than cat, honestly: loyal, clingy and very protective of her. I love crunchy treats, my little raccoon toy and chasing a string, and I fetch. My favourite thing is climbing trees whenever we go to the park.\n- On what does, what do, who is, who's: Anoushka is a UX/UI designer at Deploy in San Francisco, where she works on the product and its design system. She focuses on thoughtful, scalable products from 0–1 and beyond.\n</scripted_answers>";
